# ============================================================================
# XRayLabTool .gitignore
#
# Two rules of thumb when editing this file (borrowed from rheox's .gitignore):
#   * ANCHOR directory rules with a leading slash unless you genuinely mean
#     "at any depth". A bare `cache/` or `lib/` swallows xraylabtool/**/cache/
#     or xraylabtool/**/lib/ too, which is how a source package can vanish from
#     a commit without anyone noticing.
#   * Say WHY next to anything non-obvious, and delete a rule when nothing
#     produces its target any more: a rule with no object is a trap for whoever
#     adds a path by that name.
#
# Check a path with:  git check-ignore -v <path>
# ============================================================================

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python bytecode & compiled artifacts
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[codz]
*$py.class
*.so
*.dylib
*.dll

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environments & secrets
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `.env` not `.env*`: the latter also swallows a committed `.env.example`.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
.envrc
.venv/
venv/
# Anchored: a bare `env/` would also match any source directory of that name.
# `make clean-all` removes exactly these root-level names.
/env/
/ENV/
env.bak/
venv.bak/
.conda/
.uv-cache/
# uv writes this on `uv python pin`; CI selects the interpreter from its matrix.
.python-version

# Never commit certificate or key material, or credential dumps that a
# misnamed data file might contain (gitleaks in pre-commit is the backstop).
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
credentials*
*credentials.json
*credentials.yaml
*credentials.yml

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dependency management
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# uv.lock IS tracked: it is the single source of truth for dependencies.
# Lockfiles of other tools would only drift from it.
Pipfile.lock
poetry.lock
pdm.lock
.pdm.toml
.pdm-python
.pdm-build/
.pixi/
pixi.lock
__pypackages__/

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build, distribution & packaging
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Python
build/
dist/
downloads/
eggs/
.eggs/
develop-eggs/
sdist/
wheels/
share/python-wheels/
*.egg-info/
.installed.cfg
*.egg
*.whl
MANIFEST
pip-log.txt
pip-delete-this-directory.txt
.pypirc
*.manifest
*.spec
# No lib/, lib64/, var/ or parts/ rules: this project builds with setuptools via
# pyproject.toml (no distutils install tree, no buildout), and the bare forms
# would match any xraylabtool/**/lib/ package added later.

